# The Play *Thookkumedai* in Tiruchirappalli
Tamil title: திருச்சியில் “தூக்குமேடை” நாடகம்

Our country greatly needs plays of this kind, containing progressive ideas for the nation and society. Since this play is filled with the principles of the Dravidar Kazhagam, it may be regarded as a propaganda play for the movement. To some extent, that must indeed be conceded.

But my desire is that plays be composed not merely to explain the principles of one party, but generally to remove the defects and cruelties in society, and that comrades of every party and none should stage them.

It is clear that the country offers great support for plays containing ideas of social reform and that the people expect such plays. Yet some companies continue to perform the old mythological dramas, thereby seeking harm both for themselves and the country.

I saw an advertisement in today's newspaper. I read that a troupe called the "Netaji Drama Sabha" was going to perform *Krishna Leela*. I believe many Congress friends are present in this gathering, and I wish to bring Netaji's heroic life briefly to their memory.

He was a great martyr and hero who dedicated his entire life to the liberation of the nation. If a troupe bearing his name performs *Krishna Leela*, should Congress friends not feel ashamed? Should they not become angry at this condition? Should they not stand before that company and conduct a nonviolent picket, advising its owners: either change the company's name or perform plays that elevate society?

The Netaji Drama Sabha need not perform *Thookkumedai*. It could stage the story of the Rani of Jhansi. It could depict the Dandi March. It could present a play called *The Blood Shed by Bhagat Singh*, or *The Murder of Kumaran*, or show the Salt Satyagraha. If all these were exhausted and no other story remained, it could at least construct one around Sardar Vedarathinam.

Instead, should a drama company named after that great hero perform *Krishna Leela*? There is, however, a reason for doing so. Its owners know that "Netaji" is a marketable label. They also know that staging *Krishna Leela* will gather money from ordinary people. This is merely an attempt to combine the two and earn profit; it demonstrates neither Congress devotion nor the growth of art.

Until now, we have shown no "real men" upon our stage. We have shown only imaginary forms invisible to the eye and beyond reason. Indra has appeared, Kubera has come, and we have seen Hanuman. We have seen Alli and Pavalakkodi and Mother Sita in the Ashoka grove. In most plays we have met Narada.

But have we shown the country's great heroes, those who invaded it, those who resisted them, or the great figures of the world? Have you seen the heroic Alexander and Purushottama? At one point in this play, our friend Karunanidhi mentioned "Socrates, who died after drinking poison." Have you seen that figure upon the stage?

Two thousand years ago, that old man stood in the market streets of Greece and taught the truth. Listening to the conspirators of the town, they imprisoned and poisoned him. The whole world knows this. Have we introduced him to our country through the stage?

Why speak only of theatre? It is difficult to see pictures of such people even in our history books. Instead, we see a picture of crowned Rama standing with Sita beside him, brother Lakshmana four feet away, and a deer hidden behind a bush, with hesitation upon Rama's face and longing upon Sita's. How long is this condition to continue?

Why should reforming theatre be regarded as the work of one particular party? I tell my Congress friends: take up and conduct this work yourselves. There are innumerable scenes that ought to be shown upon our stage.

I am not asking you to reproduce the ceremony in this play in which Periyar's portrait is unveiled. I ask Congress friends why they should not stage plays explaining a widow's tears, why conflict arises between elder and younger brothers, what causes estrangement between father and son, why a good man becomes bad, and how compassion exists even in a murderer's heart.

Just as today's government has created a Censor Board for films, it has dared to control dramatic art and thereby obstruct the growth of knowledge. You may have seen in the newspaper two days ago the report that it intends to establish a "Drama Scrutiny Committee."

It has already imposed bans. It prohibited *Sigappi Who Guarded the Red Flag*, a play by friends of the Communist Party, and stopped a similar Telugu play. It banned plays of our movement, *Hiranyan, or the Peerless Hero* and *The Sword of War*.

Congress friends know of the "Song of the Crane" that Viswanatha Das sang upon the stage, how British imperialism prohibited it, how the actor defied that ban, and the consequences. Should Congress friends not feel ashamed to see a democratic government behave in the same imperial manner? Should people with artistic hearts not consider where it will lead if the government is allowed to continue imposing such prohibitions?

Are these truly the plays that ought to be banned? The story *Sabapathi* has appeared both as a stage play and a film. How disgracefully it portrays the Tamil teacher! The Tamil teacher comes into the classroom and falls asleep while the children tear and throw paper. Does that help Tamil to grow? Such stories escape the government's eyes, but it sees *Hiranyan*.

What reason did the government give for banning *Hiranyan*? This play, written fifteen years ago by the revolutionary poet Bharathidasan, has been performed many times in many places. Ministers of the period, such as P. T. Rajan, presided over its performances. Why ban such a play now?

They may say that in some places the play insults and denounces Aryans. Even assuming that it does, why should this government become angry? Why should anyone else become angry?

Whenever we speak about the Aryan-Dravidian question, Brahmin intellectuals say: "Everything has become mixed. There is no longer a separate race called Aryan." If there is honesty or truth in that claim, why should Brahmins become angry with the play?

If it is true that the Aryan race no longer exists, there is no reason for anyone to become angry when Aryans are condemned. If a play insulted or condemned the Japanese, a Japanese person watching it might become angry, or his spy might become angry. Would anyone else? In the same way, when a play condemns Aryans, only Aryans or their agents can become angry.

The play was banned. News arrived today that people staged it at Tiruvathipuram near Kanchipuram in defiance of the prohibition.

Friends, stage reformist plays for the country's progress. Stage them not only in sheds like this one, but in the open air where people may watch free of charge.

They need not all be propaganda plays one hundred per cent like this. Friends such as Karunanidhi may stage plays of that kind. Members of other parties and lovers of art need not go even that far; they may present "light-coloured" plays.

Five or six years ago I wrote *Chandrodayam* expressly as a propaganda play. I have now written the lighter-coloured *Or Iravu* and *Velaikkari*. Plays of this kind can be staged by everyone...

---NOTES---
The first body line is damaged before “நாட்டுக்கும், சமுதாயத்திற்கும்”; the translation follows the surviving parallel construction. The OCR corrupts the English parenthesis after “லைட் கலர்,” but the Tamil phrase clearly means lighter, less explicitly propagandistic drama. The sixth and final scan ends mid-sentence at “இப்படிப்பட்ட நாடகங்கள் எல்லோராலும்,” and no ending has been invented. Titles of historical plays are translated while their Tamil forms remain in the verbatim source.
